// Hypen deferred HTTP capability — v0.1.0
//
// Outbound HTTP for a *synchronous* reducer. The module does not wait for the
// response: `send` QUEUES a request and names one of the module's own actions;
// the reducer returns; the host performs the request asynchronously and
// dispatches that action back to the module with the outcome as its payload.
// The same shape as the `delay` effect ("do X, then dispatch Y") and as
// `hypen:engine/state.subscribe` (an action named by string is the callback).
//
// Why this exists next to `wasi:http` (which a module may still import
// directly, see `hypen:module`'s `module-x` world):
//   - a blocking `wasi:http` call only works on hosts that can suspend a
//     synchronous guest (native wasmtime). A browser host cannot: the reducer
//     traps. Deferred requests work on every host and need no async ABI.
//   - a blocking call holds the engine for the duration of the exchange; a
//     deferred one holds it only for the two reducer runs.
//
// Grant: the manifest's `http: true`, the same master grant as `wasi:http`.
// A module that imports this interface without the grant is refused at
// instantiation (link-time denial). `httpHosts` in the manifest optionally
// narrows the grant to an allowlist of hosts; it is checked when a request is
// queued (`permission-denied` names the host) and again by the performing
// host on every redirect hop.
//
// Completion contract. Exactly one completion is dispatched for every request
// `send` accepted, as the action `then` of the module that queued it. Its
// payload is a JSON object (action payloads are JSON):
//
//   success — the exchange completed and a response was read:
//     {
//       "ok": true,
//       "status": 200,                       // u16; ANY status, see below
//       "headers": { "content-type": "…" },  // names lowercased; a header
//                                            // sent more than once is joined
//                                            // with ", " in arrival order
//       "body": "…",                         // see bodyEncoding
//       "bodyEncoding": "utf8" | "base64",   // "utf8": the body bytes are
//                                            // valid UTF-8 and `body` is that
//                                            // text; "base64": they are not,
//                                            // and `body` is standard base64
//                                            // (RFC 4648, padded)
//       "tag": "…" | null                    // the `tag` given to `send`
//     }
//
//   failure — no response was obtained:
//     {
//       "ok": false,
//       "error": {
//         "kind": "network"            // DNS, connect, TLS, I/O
//               | "timeout"            // the request's time budget ran out
//               | "denied"             // refused after `send` accepted it:
//                                      // a redirect to a host outside
//                                      // `httpHosts`, the in-flight limit,
//                                      // a host that performs no requests
//               | "too-large"          // the response body exceeds the host's
//                                      // limit
//               | "invalid-response",  // not HTTP, or a malformed response
//         "message": "…"               // human-readable; not for matching
//       },
//       "tag": "…" | null
//     }
//
// A non-2xx status is NOT a failure: `404` or `500` arrive as `ok: true` with
// that `status` — the HTTP exchange completed, and what the status means is
// the module's business. `ok: false` means there is no response at all.
//
// A module that is destroyed or reloaded while requests are in flight does
// not receive their completions.
//
// Like `hypen:engine`, this package is a leaf: it declares its own types
// instead of using `hypen:module/types`.

package hypen:http@0.1.0;

interface deferred {
    /// An HTTP request for the host to perform.
    record request {
        /// The request method: an HTTP token (`GET`, `POST`, …). Sent as
        /// given; methods are case-sensitive.
        method: string,
        /// Absolute `http://` or `https://` URL, ASCII only (percent-encode
        /// anything else; internationalized hosts in punycode). No userinfo
        /// (`user:pass@`); a fragment is not sent.
        url: string,
        /// Request headers, in order. Names are case-insensitive. Headers the
        /// host owns are refused: `host`, `content-length`, `connection`,
        /// `transfer-encoding`, `keep-alive`, `upgrade`, `te`, `trailer`,
        /// `expect`, `proxy-*`.
        headers: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        /// Request body; `none` sends no body.
        body: option<list<u8>>,
        /// Time budget for the whole exchange, redirects included, in
        /// milliseconds. `none` is the host's default (30 s); hosts cap it
        /// (120 s). `0` is refused.
        timeout-ms: option<u32>,
    }

    variant error {
        /// The request may not be made. Payload says why and names what to
        /// change: the URL's host when it is outside the manifest's
        /// `httpHosts`, or `http` when called where requests cannot be queued
        /// (`init`, `on-destroyed`, `actions`: exports that return no
        /// reducer output to carry one).
        permission-denied(string),
        /// The request is malformed: bad method, URL, header or timeout, or
        /// `then` is not an action name. Payload says which part and why.
        invalid-request(string),
        /// A bound was exceeded: too many requests in one call, or a URL,
        /// header block or body over the size limit. Payload names the limit.
        limit-exceeded(string),
    }

    /// Queue `req`. Returns as soon as it is queued — nothing has been sent
    /// yet, and `ok` says only that the request was accepted. When it
    /// completes or fails the host dispatches the action `then` to THIS
    /// module, with the completion payload documented at the top of this
    /// file; `tag` is echoed back in it so one handler can tell requests
    /// apart.
    ///
    /// `then` must be one of the module's own actions (its `actions` export);
    /// a request naming any other action is refused when the reducer returns.
    /// Requests queued by a reducer that then returns an error are discarded.
    /// Callable from `dispatch`, `on-activated` and `on-deactivated`.
    ///
    /// Bounds (per call unless noted): 16 requests; URL 8 KiB; 64 headers and
    /// 16 KiB of header names + values; body 1 MiB; `then` and `tag` 256 bytes
    /// each. Across calls, a module may have 32 requests in flight.
    send: func(req: request, then: string, tag: option<string>) -> result<_, error>;
}
